package outlayer:tasks@0.1.0;

/// Tasks between an agent and its owner.
///
/// A run of a project, admitted to an owner's secret row, leaves a task for
/// that owner: "confirm this email", "give me your photo". The owner reads it
/// in their inbox, with no run, and approves it with one signature of their
/// wallet; the platform then starts a run of the AGENT that prepared the
/// task, on the agent's own payment key and wallet, in the operation the
/// task names, and that run carries the task out. Nothing waits inside a
/// run: a task is a record.
///
/// A project that uses tasks says so in its `outlayer.manifest`:
///
///     "tasks": true
///
/// **Whose task.** The owner of a task is the owner of the secret row the run
/// named and the keystore opened — never an account the component names. The
/// preparer is the account that made the run. A task opened by a run that
/// answered one continues that task's conversation (`thread`); its preparer
/// is the run's caller, which for the run that carries a task out is the
/// preparer again. The host decides both from the job; no function here
/// takes an account.
///
/// **The consent.** A task is carried out by a run of its preparer that the
/// platform starts on the owner's approval, paid by the payment key that
/// made the preparing run, with its wallet, its identity and within its
/// compute limit. Those facts are sealed with the task in `open` as the
/// preparer's consent to that run, so `open` is refused `no-payment-key` in
/// a run that has none: a task is opened over HTTPS with a payment key.
///
/// **A notice** (`kind: notice`) tells the owner something and asks nothing:
/// it names no operation to answer by, nothing is written back to it, and no
/// run follows it — so it carries no consent, and a run with no payment key,
/// one made on chain included, may open one. The owner closes it with Got
/// it, and its preparer reads it `done`; `answered` refuses it
/// `answer-invalid`. Everything else is a task's: who may open one, the
/// limits, the life, what it shows, the files, the proof.
///
/// **Who may do what.**
///
/// - `open`: a run admitted to the owner's row BY NAME — the row's condition
///   lists the caller — or the owner's own run. A row open to everyone, to a
///   pattern or to holders of a token admits the run and refuses the task.
/// - `mine`, `status`, `cancel`, `delete`: the tasks whose preparer is this
///   caller in this project for this owner, and no others.
/// - `answered`, `report`: the run of the preparer the platform started on
///   the owner's approval — the same account, payment key, wallet and
///   identity as the preparing run, within its compute limit — and no other
///   run, the owner's own included.
/// - `unlock`: the owner's own run.
///
/// A run of another project reaches nothing of this project's tasks: they are
/// sealed under a key bound to the project and the owner, which the host
/// holds and the component never sees.
///
/// **What a task shows** is a structure the owner's page draws as plain text:
/// a title and fields, each a label, a kind from a fixed list and its values.
/// No markup is interpreted, no link can be pressed, nothing is loaded. The
/// host checks the structure in `open`; one outside the bounds refuses the
/// task.
///
/// **What follows an approval is code.** A task names the operation that
/// carries it out (`answer-by`) and carries no arguments for it. The
/// platform starts the preparer's run in that operation with an input of
/// `task_id`, `task_hash`, `approval`, and `supplied` and `note` when the
/// owner wrote them; the operation calls `answered` with them, takes back
/// the `state` it sealed when it made the task, acts, and calls `report`.
///
/// **States.** `open` → `approved` (the owner signed; the platform started
/// the preparer's run) → `answering` (that run took the task) → `done` (it
/// ended well and reported) or `failed` (it ended any other way, or was
/// refused the task before it took it, or could not be started). Or `open`
/// → `rejected`, `cancelled`, `expired`, `void`. A notice is `open` →
/// `done` (the owner saw it), `cancelled` or `expired`. Each move is made
/// once. A task never returns to `open`.
///
/// **Absence is a value, failure an error.** `mine` with nothing answers
/// `ok([])`. A store or a chain that did not answer is `err` with
/// `unavailable` — never an empty list. Every error carries a `reason` the
/// component branches on and a `message` for a person; no function traps.
interface api {
    /// What a task asks of the owner.
    enum task-kind {
        /// Yes or no.
        confirm,
        /// The owner supplies something.
        input,
        /// Tells the owner something; asks nothing.
        notice,
    }

    /// How the owner's page draws a field's values.
    enum field-kind {
        money,
        account,
        address,
        text,
        long-text,
        %list,
    }

    /// Whose words a field's values are.
    enum written-by {
        /// Computed or fetched by the project's code: an amount, a payee's
        /// name as the bank gives it.
        project,
        /// Written by the agent that asked: an email body, a memo.
        agent,
    }

    /// One field of what a task shows. `values` holds exactly one value,
    /// except for `list`, which holds 1 to 20.
    record field {
        label: string,
        kind: field-kind,
        values: list<string>,
        written-by: written-by,
    }

    /// What the owner is shown: a title of at most 80 characters and at most
    /// 12 fields. A label is at most 40 characters; a value at most 50000 in
    /// a `long-text`, 500 in a `text`, 200 anywhere else. Refused: control
    /// characters, characters that are not drawn or that reorder or hide
    /// text, more than four combining marks in a row, and a text made only
    /// of spaces. A `long-text` may hold line breaks and tabs. An emoji is
    /// written with its selector and its joiner, and is drawn.
    record display {
        title: string,
        fields: list<field>,
    }

    /// A file that is part of what the owner is asked about: an attachment
    /// of a message, a document to sign off. The owner's page lists it by
    /// name, type and size and gives it to the owner to open, as a download;
    /// `answered` hands it back. At most 10 files a task, 6291456 bytes
    /// together; a name of at most 200 characters, a type of at most 100.
    /// A name is no path, neither begins nor ends with a space or a dot, and
    /// is of one file of the task, whatever its case; a type is a type and a
    /// subtype about one `/`.
    record file {
        name: string,
        content-type: string,
        data: list<u8>,
    }

    /// What the owner supplies with their answer.
    enum supplies {
        nothing,
        text,
        /// A reference to a file and its hash; the file itself is elsewhere.
        file,
    }

    /// How the owner answers: the operation of this project they call
    /// (`[a-z0-9_]`, at most 64), and what they supply with it.
    record answer-by {
        operation: string,
        supplies: supplies,
    }

    /// A task to open.
    record request {
        kind: task-kind,
        display: display,
        /// The operation that carries the owner's answer out: present for a
        /// `confirm` and an `input`, absent for a `notice`. Either way round
        /// is refused `display-invalid`.
        answer-by: option<answer-by>,
        /// The files the owner is shown beside the fields.
        files: list<file>,
        /// Bytes of the component's own, at most 262144, sealed beside the
        /// task and handed back by `answered`. The owner is never shown them.
        state: list<u8>,
        /// The policy the task is made under, as the component reads it; at
        /// most 65536 bytes. `answered` is given the policy of that moment,
        /// and a task made under another is `void`.
        policy: list<u8>,
        /// How long the task waits, in seconds. 0 is the longest a task may
        /// wait; more than that is refused `life-too-long`. A task that takes
        /// an answer waits 900 at least; less is refused `display-invalid`.
        life-seconds: u32,
    }

    /// A task that was opened.
    record opened {
        id: string,
        /// SHA-256 of the task's envelope, hex: what the owner's answer names.
        hash: string,
        /// The id of the first task of the conversation: this task's own, or
        /// that of the task this run answered, whose preparer it keeps.
        thread: string,
        /// Unix seconds.
        expires-at: u64,
        /// How many of the owner's devices read it at once. 0: the owner
        /// reads it after signing in and one run of `unlock`.
        devices: u32,
    }

    enum task-state {
        open,
        /// The owner approved; the preparer's run is started and has not yet
        /// taken the task.
        approved,
        answering,
        done,
        failed,
        rejected,
        cancelled,
        expired,
        void,
    }

    /// A task as its preparer reads it.
    record outcome {
        id: string,
        kind: task-kind,
        state: task-state,
        /// Unix seconds.
        created-at: u64,
        expires-at: u64,
        /// The run that acts, or acted, on the owner's approval: present on
        /// `approved`, `answering`, `done` and `failed`. Its status is the
        /// platform's ordinary status of a call.
        run: option<string>,
        /// What the project reported: on `done`, the result; on `failed` as
        /// `run_trapped`, the result it reported before the run failed; as
        /// `run_failed`, why it was not carried out.
        %result: option<list<u8>>,
        /// The owner's reason, as they wrote it: on `rejected`, when they
        /// gave one.
        rejection: option<string>,
        /// Why a task is `failed`. Before its run acted: `preparer_key_unavailable`
        /// (the payment key that prepared could not pay for the run),
        /// `operation_priced`, `operation_unknown`, `operation_limit_reached`,
        /// `wallet_unresolved`, `queue_unavailable`, `run_not_started`, or
        /// `run_refused:<reason>` with the reason the host refused the run
        /// `answered` with — `run_refused:unreported` when the run ended
        /// without ever asking for the task. After its run took the answer:
        /// `run_failed` (the project said, with `report-failure`, that it did
        /// not carry the task out; why is in `result`), `run_trapped` (it
        /// reported the task carried out, and then the run failed; what it
        /// reported is in `result`), `run_unreported` (the run ended without a
        /// word on the task — it may have acted),
        /// `run_unfinished` (no word of the run's end in time — it may have
        /// acted). Absent in every other state.
        failure-reason: option<string>,
    }

    /// The owner's approval of a task: one NEP-413 signature their wallet
    /// made over a sentence naming the task's id, the hash of what they were
    /// shown and the digest of what they supplied and wrote, at a minute.
    /// Handed to the run in its input by the platform and passed to
    /// `answered` as it is; the host rebuilds the sentence from what is
    /// sealed and verifies the signature under a full-access key of the
    /// owner's account on chain.
    record approval {
        /// Unix seconds: the minute the sentence names.
        at: u64,
        /// The key that signed, `ed25519:<base58>`.
        public-key: string,
        /// Base64, 64 bytes.
        signature: string,
        /// Base64, 32 bytes.
        nonce: string,
    }

    /// What `answered` hands the operation that acts.
    record answer {
        id: string,
        thread: string,
        /// The account whose run made the task.
        preparer: string,
        kind: task-kind,
        /// The operation the task named: the one running.
        operation: string,
        /// What the component sealed in `open`.
        state: list<u8>,
        /// The files the task was opened with, as the owner was given them.
        files: list<file>,
        /// What the owner supplied, opened: text, or a file's reference.
        /// Absent for a task that asked for nothing.
        supplied: option<list<u8>>,
        /// What the owner wrote beside their approval, opened: text of at
        /// most 50000 characters. Absent when they wrote nothing.
        note: option<list<u8>>,
    }

    enum reason {
        /// The manifest does not say `"tasks": true`.
        not-declared,
        /// The run names no secret row, the row was not opened, or the run
        /// does not go through a project.
        no-owner,
        /// The run was relayed: the account that called OutLayer is not the
        /// account that signed. A contract the signer called made this run,
        /// so nothing here is the signer's own act.
        relayed,
        /// The row admitted the run by a rule that does not list the caller.
        not-granted-by-name,
        /// The owner muted this agent or this project.
        muted,
        /// The owner's limit of open tasks, this preparer's share of it, or
        /// the owner's limit of what waiting tasks may hold together.
        inbox-full,
        /// This run opened as many tasks, or made as many calls of this
        /// interface, as one run may. Another run starts from nothing.
        run-limit,
        /// A kind outside the list, too many fields, a value too long, or a
        /// life too short for a task that takes an answer: the message names
        /// which.
        display-invalid,
        /// `state`, `policy`, the files, the envelope or a result over its
        /// bound.
        too-large,
        /// A life asked beyond the maximum.
        life-too-long,
        /// No such task of this project for this owner, or for this preparer.
        not-found,
        /// `unlock` in a run that is not the owner's.
        not-the-owner,
        /// `answered` in a run that is not the preparer's run the consent
        /// names: another account, another payment key, another wallet,
        /// another identity, or more compute than the preparing run allowed.
        /// The message names which.
        not-the-preparer,
        /// The approval does not hold: its time is outside ten minutes of
        /// now or past the task's life, its sentence is not this task's, its
        /// signature does not verify, or the key that signed is not a
        /// full-access key of the owner's account. The message names which.
        approval-invalid,
        /// `open` of a task that takes an answer in a run that has no payment
        /// key: the consent to carry out the owner's answer is a payment key
        /// — the run that carries it out is paid by that key — so such a
        /// task is opened over HTTPS with one. A notice needs none.
        no-payment-key,
        /// The hash named is not the task's.
        hash-mismatch,
        /// What the owner supplied is not what the task asked for, or does
        /// not open; or the task is a notice, which takes no answer.
        answer-invalid,
        /// The task was answered, rejected or cancelled already.
        closed,
        /// Past its life.
        expired,
        /// The policy changed since the task was made, or the run that
        /// answers is of another build of the project than the one that
        /// made it: what the owner was shown and proved is what acts.
        void,
        /// A sealed copy that does not open. The message is `decryption
        /// failed` and nothing more.
        unreadable,
        /// The store or the chain did not answer. The same call may succeed
        /// later.
        unavailable,
        /// A fault of the platform's own: the host or the store could not do
        /// what was asked, and the same call will not do better. The task's
        /// id, where one is named, was taken by an earlier run of this call.
        /// Also `open` in a run that answered tasks of more than one
        /// conversation: a task it opens belongs to none.
        internal,
    }

    record task-error {
        reason: reason,
        message: string,
    }

    /// Open a task for the owner of the secret row this run was admitted to,
    /// with this run's consent to the run that will carry it out.
    open: func(request: request) -> result<opened, task-error>;

    /// The tasks whose preparer is this caller in this project for this
    /// owner: the open ones, and the outcomes kept.
    mine: func() -> result<list<outcome>, task-error>;

    /// One of them.
    status: func(id: string) -> result<outcome, task-error>;

    /// Take the task `id`, approved by its owner, whose envelope hashes to
    /// `hash`, in the operation `operation` — the one now running; a task
    /// that names another to answer by is refused `answer-invalid` and stays
    /// as it is. `policy` is the policy as the component reads it now;
    /// `approval` the owner's approval as the run's input carries it;
    /// `supplied` and `note` what the owner's page sent, each sealed to the
    /// task's reply key, as the input carries them. The run must be the one
    /// the task's consent names (`not-the-preparer`), the approval must hold
    /// (`approval-invalid`), and the task must be `approved`. On `ok` the
    /// task is `answering` under this run, and what it showed is deleted from
    /// the store. A refusal of a task that is `approved` is recorded and,
    /// when the run ends, fails the task with `run_refused:<reason>`.
    answered: func(id: string, hash: string, operation: string, policy: list<u8>, approval: approval, supplied: option<list<u8>>, note: option<list<u8>>) -> result<answer, task-error>;

    /// Leave `result` (at most 16384 bytes) for the preparer of a task this
    /// run answered, and with it the word that the task was carried out: it
    /// is `done` when the run ends as a success, and `failed` as
    /// `run_trapped`, `result` kept, when the run fails after. A task this run
    /// answered and reported nothing of is `failed` as `run_unreported`.
    report: func(id: string, %result: list<u8>) -> result<_, task-error>;

    /// Leave `result` (at most 16384 bytes) for the preparer of a task this
    /// run answered, with the word that it was NOT carried out: the task is
    /// `failed` as `run_failed` when the run ends, and `result` — the
    /// project's refusal — is what the preparer reads. A later `report` or
    /// `report-failure` of the same task replaces it.
    report-failure: func(id: string, %result: list<u8>) -> result<_, task-error>;

    /// Withdraw an open task whose preparer is this caller.
    cancel: func(id: string) -> result<_, task-error>;

    /// Delete a task whose preparer is this caller and that nothing was
    /// carried out on: open (withdrawn with it), cancelled, rejected,
    /// expired, void, or failed with a reason that says nothing was done —
    /// `run_failed`, `run_not_started`, `run_refused:*`, or a run that was
    /// never started. One the owner's yes acted on, or may have — approved,
    /// answering, done, failed for any other reason — is the owner's record
    /// too, and is refused `closed`.
    delete: func(id: string) -> result<_, task-error>;

    /// Write the copies of this project's open tasks for every device the
    /// owner has in force — for a device that was not signed in when the
    /// tasks were made. Answers how many tasks were opened for a device,
    /// whatever the number of devices; the owner's page reads them from the
    /// inbox, and nothing of them passes through the component.
    unlock: func() -> result<u32, task-error>;
}

world tasks-host {
    import api;
}
